   "evidence": "The proof uses only `isFiniteMeasure_restrict`, `MemLp.mono_exponent`, `integrable_norm_rpow`, `setAverage_eq`, `integral_eq_zero_iff_of_nonneg_ae`, `measureReal_ball_pos` and `Real.rpow_eq_zero`. The ball is used only for finite, positive measure. `Average.lean` already states its set-average lemmas for an arbitrary set with `\u03bc s \u2260 0` and `\u03bc s \u2260 \u22a4`, e.g. `sq_setAverage_norm_le_setAverage_norm_sq` (l.344) and `enorm_setAverage_sub_le_of_subset` (l.375).",
   "file": "TauCeti/Analysis/PDE/MaximumPrinciple/Strong.lean",
   "fix": "Move it, public, to `TauCeti/MeasureTheory/Integral/Average.lean` (namespace `TauCeti`). State it for a set `s` with `\u03bc s \u2260 0` and `\u03bc s \u2260 \u22a4`, with `0 < p` and either `IntegrableOn (g \u00b7 ^ p) s \u03bc` or `MemLp g q` with `p \u2264 q`. At the call site, discharge `\u03bc (ball y (2*r)) \u2260 0` and `\u2260 \u22a4` with `measure_ball_pos` and `measure_ball_lt_top`.",
   "issue": "`ae_eq_zero_of_setAverage_rpow_eq_zero` is a general result about set averages, but it is `private` and tied to balls in `EuclideanSpace` and to `MemLp g 2` with `p \u2264 2`. Its statement uses only Mathlib types and its proof never uses the maximum principle.",
